# The contract version, and the document it names.
#
# One record per contract version, in the order they shipped, appended and never edited:
#
#     <version>  sha256:<digest of tools.json with its own `contract` field removed>
#
# `tools.json` declares the version, `VERSION` states it, `README.md`'s capability block
# prints it and `docs/mcp-server.md`'s handshake example shows it. All four were held to each
# other and none of them was required to move, so two branches could each add a tool, each
# ship `"contract": "0.24.0"`, and every gate stay green on both and on the merge (#940). This
# file is the half that was missing: a number, once written here, is that document's name, and
# a second change claiming it has to write a second line for a version already in the file.
#
# The digest is over the WHOLE document minus the version — prose included. Measured against
# this file's own history: 27 commits to `tools.json` carrying 25 bumps, of which 11 changed
# no structured field at all. `0.16.0 → 0.17.0` specified what an ordering does when two dates
# disagree about precision, entirely in two `notes` paragraphs. The frozen vocabularies live in
# that prose too, deliberately. A digest over names, tiers and schemas would have been silent
# on those bumps, which is most of what this contract has ever changed.
#
# Appending is part of a bump, and `yidam/cli/tests/mcp_contract_digest.rs` is what says so:
# it recomputes the digest of the contract in the tree, fails when the last record disagrees,
# and prints the line to append. Reformatting `tools.json` is free — keys are sorted and
# whitespace dropped before hashing — and a reworded sentence is not: the contract is the
# document, and a client that read 0.24.0 yesterday must not get different bytes today under
# that number.
#
# The 25 records before the last were measured, not written: each is the digest of the
# `tools.json` a commit left behind, and
# `the_ledger_is_the_history_the_repository_records` recomputes all of them from the history
# whenever it is run against a full clone. 0.16.0 stood two ways — `948c316` reworded a `notes`
# paragraph without moving the version, which is this hole happening once — and the record is
# the document as that version ended.
